# NEOCOM workspace

Agent configuration lives in `neocom-meta` (`neocom/neocom-meta`). Product repos are **git submodules** of the parent `neocom` repo; the parent tracks their SHAs via submodule pointers and stays synchronized with `git submodule update --init --recursive` (or clone with `--recurse-submodules`).

| Folder | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `neocom-meta` | Docs, programme definition, Cursor rules/skills, `02.SOURCES`, and subagents (submodule) |
| `neocom-front` | Frontend application — Angular specialist agent ([AdamAntinoo/neocom-front](https://github.com/AdamAntinoo/neocom-front.git)) (submodule) |
| `neocom-domain` | Business-domain TypeScript library — Node library agent ([AdamAntinoo/neocom-domain](https://github.com/AdamAntinoo/neocom-domain.git)) |
| `neocom-esi` | ESI TypeScript library and tools ([AdamAntinoo/neocom-esi](https://github.com/AdamAntinoo/neocom-esi.git)). Releases: `npm run release`, commits via `/neocom-git` |
| `neocom-back` | Backend application (when present; expected as a submodule) |

## Front stack

- Latest Angular + TypeScript
- Unit tests: Vitest (Angular CLI default)
- Acceptance tests: Cypress with Cucumber

## Ecosystem tools

At the `neocom` root (parent of `neocom-meta`, `neocom-front`, `neocom-back`):

- **OpenSpec** — spec-driven planning and change workflow (`openspec` CLI; Cursor integration via `openspec init --tools cursor`)
- **Graphify** — code-knowledge graph for Cursor (`graphify` CLI from package `graphifyy`)

See `.cursor/rules/neocom-ecosystem-tools.mdc` for install and usage notes.

## Delegation

Use these project subagents proactively:

- `/neocom-front` — Angular UI and client work in `neocom-front` (Angular specialist)
- `/neocom-domain` — Node library, GitVersion, and npm publish in `neocom-domain`
- `/neocom-back` — API and server work in `neocom-back`
- `/neocom-meta` — docs, agent config, sources, and spec pipeline in `neocom-meta`
- `/neocom-git` — commits, PRs, releases, and promotions. OpenSpec feature apply commits on `feature/<feature>` from `develop`. `neocom-esi` releases go through `npm run release`
- `/spec-collect` — Spec Collect: `02.SOURCES` → L1 under `05.REQUIREMENTS-DECOMPOSITION`
- `/neo:register-spec` — Register Requirements: review new L1/L2 notes, then register them in OpenSpec
- `/spec-registration` — same registration workflow as `/neo:register-spec`
- `/feature-delivery` — branch `features/<feature>` from `develop`, then pull request into `develop` after validation and archive
- `/verifier` — readonly check after work is claimed done

Keep each change inside the folder that owns it. Do not implement frontend features in meta or back, or backend features in meta or front.

When `neocom-front` is missing or empty, use the recorded front stack (do not re-ask). When `neocom-back` is missing or empty, ask which backend stack and repository to use before scaffolding.

Requirements flow: `/spec-collect`, then `/neo:register-spec` for new requirements, then `/feature-delivery` when applying a feature. See `.cursor/rules/neocom-spec-pipeline.mdc`.

Programme docs: keep `01.PROGRAMME-DEFINITION/` current (architecture + active features such as Initialization and Login) as inputs and development produce durable decisions.
